WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.651 --> 00:00:05.150
(soothing violin music)

00:00:05.150 --> 00:00:06.160
- [Woman] When I was growing up,

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:08.220
I spent a lot of time outside.

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:09.600
I would go on these adventures,

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:13.330
either in the local park
or even my front yard.

00:00:13.330 --> 00:00:16.230
And I spent a lot of time
searching for four leaf clovers.

00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:20.700
Science starts with observation,

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:24.000
being able to pay close
attention, and ask question.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:25.713
That's the spark of science.

00:00:26.605 --> 00:00:30.580
(soothing orchestral music)

00:00:30.580 --> 00:00:31.766
Oh! Wait a minute, here's another one!

00:00:31.766 --> 00:00:32.599
(mumbles)

00:00:32.599 --> 00:00:35.182
(woman laughs)

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:37.853
That's usually a good sign.

00:00:37.853 --> 00:00:40.783
I like to put traps where I
see a lot of four leaf clovers.

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:44.000
I'm Danielle N. Lee.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:46.510
I'm a National Geographic
Emerging Explorer,

00:00:46.510 --> 00:00:48.593
and I study nuisance rodents.

00:00:52.310 --> 00:00:55.790
A nuisance or pest animal is any animal

00:00:55.790 --> 00:00:57.713
that gives people a problem.

00:00:58.770 --> 00:01:00.220
Rodents, like rats and mice,

00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:03.420
have been a problem for humans
since the beginning of time.

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:05.550
They can cause a lot of disease,

00:01:05.550 --> 00:01:07.577
so we wanna understand more about them

00:01:07.577 --> 00:01:10.543
so that we can live
healthier, better lives.

00:01:12.630 --> 00:01:15.200
I'm interested in
studying nuisance rodents

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:18.580
because I wanna understand
how these animals

00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:21.690
successfully persist
despite how hard people

00:01:21.690 --> 00:01:25.223
have tried to get rid of
them for hundreds of years.

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:30.320
So I study field mice across
an urban to rural gradient.

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:32.790
So that's looking from a big city,

00:01:32.790 --> 00:01:34.760
all the way to a farm area,

00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:37.500
to a more natural area
where there's more forest

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:38.703
and grasslands.

00:01:40.490 --> 00:01:45.260
I'm trying to understand
the behavioral differences

00:01:45.260 --> 00:01:48.250
of city mice and country mice.

00:01:48.250 --> 00:01:51.333
(mousetrap clicking)

00:01:52.230 --> 00:01:53.500
I like these small traps

00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:55.750
because they carry well in your bag.

00:01:55.750 --> 00:01:57.750
I open them up and they are ready to go.

00:01:58.610 --> 00:02:00.040
And then when I find the perfect place

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:01.593
where I think mice may be,

00:02:01.593 --> 00:02:03.903
It's just as easy as that to set them.

00:02:04.810 --> 00:02:06.980
I come back just a couple hours later,

00:02:06.980 --> 00:02:09.230
and I can catch a mouse
if they are out here.

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:13.010
(wooden fence creaks)

00:02:13.010 --> 00:02:14.820
This is the urban part of my gradient,

00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:17.460
here in the St. Louis metro area.

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:19.400
Here surrounded by buildings,

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:22.120
there are still a lot
of places for wildlife,

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:23.270
like nuisance rodents,

00:02:23.270 --> 00:02:25.610
to find a perfect place to live

00:02:25.610 --> 00:02:26.883
and make a living.

00:02:27.872 --> 00:02:30.539
(playful music)

00:02:33.744 --> 00:02:35.493
(birds singing)

00:02:35.493 --> 00:02:37.388
(mumbles)

00:02:37.388 --> 00:02:38.290
Oh yeah.

00:02:38.290 --> 00:02:39.763
Smells like a mouse.

00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:43.570
Once we catch the mice safely,

00:02:43.570 --> 00:02:45.390
we removed him from the trap.

00:02:45.390 --> 00:02:47.053
He's swaddled like a baby.

00:02:49.352 --> 00:02:50.185
All right.

00:02:50.185 --> 00:02:52.000
So we got another Peromyscus.

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:53.370
Definitely a male.

00:02:53.370 --> 00:02:54.743
14.5 grams.

00:02:54.743 --> 00:02:55.710
We weigh them.

00:02:55.710 --> 00:02:57.960
Measure different parts of their body,

00:02:57.960 --> 00:03:01.113
and then we individually
identify them with a tag.

00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:05.450
This gives us a glimpse
into how animal communities

00:03:05.450 --> 00:03:07.903
are using habitats in our area.

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:13.610
Understanding what makes city mouse

00:03:13.610 --> 00:03:16.960
and country mouse the same and different

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:20.490
from one another is the
goal of my research.

00:03:20.490 --> 00:03:23.080
And I'm interested in
differences that might

00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:27.510
make them bolder and risk
coming into your house

00:03:27.510 --> 00:03:30.329
as opposed to staying outside.

00:03:30.329 --> 00:03:32.430
(gentle acoustic guitar music)

00:03:32.430 --> 00:03:33.760
I think understanding them better

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:37.043
is how we do better at living around them.

00:03:38.210 --> 00:03:40.450
So I'll release her back
to where she came from,

00:03:40.450 --> 00:03:43.330
and if I catch her again in the same place

00:03:43.330 --> 00:03:46.123
then I know that this is
roughly where she's made a home.

00:03:48.270 --> 00:03:52.580
I think its important to
do science close to home.

00:03:52.580 --> 00:03:55.550
Its so many questions
that we still don't know,

00:03:55.550 --> 00:03:58.020
and so many answers that we've yet to find

00:03:58.020 --> 00:04:00.820
about the wildlife that lives
right outside of our door.

00:04:01.940 --> 00:04:05.463
There are living
laboratories all around us.

00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:08.033
And you can get started now.

00:04:09.150 --> 00:04:10.010
There she goes,

00:04:11.426 --> 00:04:12.259
back home.

00:04:13.546 --> 00:04:17.379
(happy acoustic guitar music)

